==Special Enemies and Damage Attenuation==
{{UpdateMe|Need details on exact numbers and add more enemies that have some unique DR on their shields or health (separate from armor)}}
Certain enemies possess unique damage reduction calculations separate from armor or sources of pure damage reduction. Usually, this unique damage reduction scales based on the weapon's average damage per second (DPS) (more specifically burst DPS), sometimes excluding [[Critical Hit]]s. Officially, this is referred to as '''adaptive damage scaling''', '''scaling damage reduction''', or '''damage attenuation'''.<ref>https://forums.warframe.com/topic/1269749-update-305-sisters-of-parvos/</ref> The game calculates DPS typically as:
::<math>\begin{aligned}
\text{Burst DPS} &= (\text{total damage}) \\
&\qquad\cdot(\text{total critical multiplier if damage instance crits}) \\
&\qquad\cdot(\text{total fire rate or attack speed}) \\
&\qquad\cdot(\text{total multishot}) \\
&\qquad\cdot(\text{body part multipliers})
\end{aligned}</math>

Where damage, critical multiplier, fire rate/attack speed, and multishot is calculated after mods and buffs are applied (e.g. {{WF|Octavia}}'s {{A|Amp}} or {{WF|Volt}}'s {{M|Shock Trooper}} for damage, {{M|Sharpened Bullets}} for critical multiplier, {{WF|Valkyr}}'s {{A|Warcry}} or {{WF|Gauss}}' {{A|Redline}} for fire rate/attack speed, and {{M|Split Flights}} for multishot).

Note that weapons with the auto-spool trigger type, like the {{Weapon|Gorgon}} or {{Weapon|Kohm}}, will noticeably deal less damage per damage instance as their fire rate ramps up to their maximum value.

In most cases, this scaling DR stacks multiplicatively with DR provided by the enemy's [[armor]].

To calculate damage reduction based on DPS:
::<math>\text{Damage reduction percentage} = (1 - \text{Damage multiplier or modifier}) \times 100</math>

